WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.497
Let's begin with naming.

00:00:01.497 --> 00:00:07.557
Readable code starts with names because names are the first explanation a reader sees.

00:00:07.557 --> 00:00:13.987
If a variable is called x, the reader has to search through the code to understand what x

00:00:13.987 --> 00:00:19.807
means. If the same value is called monthly_sales, the meaning is much clearer.

00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:25.262
In the table on this slide, notice the difference between weak names and better names.

00:00:25.262 --> 00:00:29.482
The name df2 might mean something to the person who wrote it,

00:00:29.482 --> 00:00:34.872
but it does not tell a future reader much.

00:00:34.872 --> 00:00:41.267
A name like clean_orders tells us that the data probably contains order records and that some cleaning step

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:41.812
has already happened.

00:00:41.812 --> 00:00:49.182
The code example uses names such as monthly_sales, month_count, and average_monthly_sales.

00:00:49.182 --> 00:00:53.142
These names are a little longer, but they are easier to understand.

00:00:53.142 --> 00:00:59.352
Before you run the cell, try to predict what it will print.

00:00:59.352 --> 00:01:05.422
The code adds the monthly sales, divides by the number of months, and prints the average.

00:01:05.422 --> 00:01:10.402
After running it, try changing one of the sales values and run it again.

00:01:10.402 --> 00:01:16.720
A good naming habit is this: choose names that describe the role of a value in the analysis.
